---
import DefaultLayout from '@/layouts/DefaultLayout.astro';

// main has no shared constants module; native-docs imports this from @/constants.
const CONTACT_EMAIL = 'contact@atlasos.net';

const lastUpdated = 'October 16, 2026';

const linkedData = [
  {
    data: 'Level and XP',
    why: 'The leveling system and level roles. Kept after you leave the server so your level and roles are restored if you rejoin.',
    kept: 'Until you ask us to delete it.',
  },
  {
    data: "Moderation cases: your user ID, the moderator's user ID, the action (warning, timeout, kick, ban, unban, removal of a timeout), the reason given, and when it happened",
    why: 'Moderation history, appeals and keeping the server safe.',
    kept: 'As long as needed to keep the server safe (see Moderation records).',
  },
  {
    data: 'Per-moderator action counts (how many bans, kicks, timeouts and so on each moderator issued)',
    why: 'Staff workload and transparency statistics.',
    kept: 'Until deletion, when merged into a total labelled Deleted, unless retained for moderation (see below).',
  },
  {
    data: 'Invite attribution: which invite code you joined with and who created it; invite codes and how often each was used',
    why: 'Shown in /userinfo; invite statistics.',
    kept: "Until you (or the invite's creator) ask us to delete it.",
  },
  {
    data: 'Message count per Wrapped season',
    why: 'Server Wrapped (yearly statistics such as the most active members). Only a number per season; no content.',
    kept: 'Until you ask us to delete it.',
  },
  {
    data: 'Starboard posts: the message ID, channel, author and star count of messages that reached the starboard',
    why: 'Avoiding duplicate starboard posts.',
    kept: 'Until you ask us to delete it.',
  },
  {
    data: 'Suggestions: the text you submit, its status, and which staff member accepted or denied it',
    why: 'The suggestions system.',
    kept: 'Until you ask us to delete it.',
  },
];
---

<DefaultLayout
  title="Discord Bot Privacy Policy | AtlasOS"
  description="Privacy policy for Atlas, the bot of the AtlasOS Discord server: what it collects, why, how long it is kept and how to have it deleted."
>
  <section class="mx-auto mt-16 max-w-4xl px-4 pb-16">
    <h1 class="mb-2 text-4xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-white md:text-5xl">Discord Bot Privacy Policy</h1>
    <p class="mb-8 text-sm text-white/50">Last updated: {lastUpdated}</p>
    <div class="space-y-8 leading-relaxed text-white/75">
      <div class="space-y-3">
        <h2 class="mb-3 text-xl font-semibold text-white">Overview</h2>
        <p>
          Atlas is the Discord bot of the{' '}
          <a class="text-primary hover:underline" href="https://discord.gg/atlasos">
            AtlasOS Discord server
          </a>
          . It is operated by the AtlasOS project ("we", "us"). This policy explains what Atlas collects, why, where it
          is kept, for how long, and how to have it corrected or deleted. For the AtlasOS website, see our{' '}
          <a class="text-primary hover:underline" href="/privacy-policy/">
            main privacy policy
          </a>
          .
        </p>
        <p>
          Atlas's server features only operate inside the AtlasOS server. Atlas also processes direct messages sent to
          it and may retain records about former members and people involved in moderation.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong class="text-white/90">Contact:</strong> email{' '}
          <a class="text-primary hover:underline" href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>
            {CONTACT_EMAIL}
          </a>{' '}
          or talk to a support representative in{' '}
          <a class="text-primary hover:underline" href="https://discord.gg/atlasos">
            discord.gg/atlasos
          </a>
          .
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="space-y-3">
        <h2 class="mb-3 text-xl font-semibold text-white">Summary</h2>
        <ul class="list-disc space-y-1 pl-6">
          <li>
            Atlas stores your <strong class="text-white/90">Discord user ID</strong> next to a small amount of data
            about your activity in the server: your level, moderation cases, which invite you joined with, and a few
            counters.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-white/90">Atlas does not keep a database archive of ordinary server messages.</strong>{' '}
            It caches messages in memory to run its features. Suggestion text, moderation reasons and staff-configured
            content are stored separately. Selected deleted/edited messages and DMs are copied to restricted Discord log
            channels (see{' '}
            <a class="text-primary hover:underline" href="#discord-log-channels">
              Discord log channels
            </a>
            ).
          </li>
          <li>
            When a member invokes <strong class="text-white/90">Jarvis</strong> or{' '}
            <strong class="text-white/90">Translate</strong>, selected text and relevant context are sent to Groq. This
            may include messages written by other members. Search links can also contain message text, which is sent to
            the linked service when someone follows the link.
          </li>
          <li>
            We do <strong class="text-white/90">not</strong> sell data, use it for advertising, or use it to train AI
            models.
          </li>
          <li>You can ask us to see, correct or delete your data at any time.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="space-y-3">
        <h2 class="mb-3 text-xl font-semibold text-white">Data Linked to Your User ID</h2>
        <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-lg border border-white/10">
          <table class="w-full text-left text-sm">
            <thead class="bg-white/5 text-white/90">
              <tr>
                <th class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">Data</th>
                <th class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">Why</th>
                <th class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">Kept for</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody class="divide-y divide-white/10">
              {linkedData.map((row) => (
                <tr class="align-top">
                  <td class="px-4 py-3">{row.data}</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-3">{row.why}</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-3">{row.kept}</td>
                </tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="space-y-3">
        <h2 class="mb-3 text-xl font-semibold text-white">Aggregate Statistics and Message Links</h2>
        <p>
          Atlas keeps counters that are not linked to any user ID, for example: how many current members have each
          onboarding status or profile badge, members by the year their Discord account was created, messages per
          monitored channel, how often configured keywords are mentioned per channel, new forum posts per tag, AutoMod
          blocks, server boosts, and the most used emojis of a Wrapped season. These counters are kept indefinitely.
          Atlas also retains individual message IDs and links with reaction counts for Wrapped. These links can lead
          back to an identifiable author; they are not anonymous totals and are not automatically removed by{' '}
          <code>/privacy delete</code>.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="space-y-3">
        <h2 class="mb-3 text-xl font-semibold text-white">Submitted and Configured Text</h2>
        <p>
          Atlas stores suggestion text and moderation reasons, including reasons received from Discord's audit log. It
          also stores staff-configured auto-responses, custom commands, scheduled messages and settings. These fields
          may contain names, mentions, quotations or other personal information. Staff-configured content remains until
          staff change or remove it. A privacy request may require manual review of text referring to you, including
          text submitted by someone else.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="space-y-3">
        <h2 class="mb-3 text-xl font-semibold text-white">Processing and Temporary Caches</h2>
        <ul class="list-disc space-y-1 pl-6">
          <li>
            <strong class="text-white/90">Ordinary message content</strong> is read in memory to run auto-responses,
            count configured keywords, and check attachments and images (file type checks and reading the text in images
            to block scams). Message activity also earns XP. The message cache holds up to 200 messages per channel; an
            hourly sweep removes messages older than six hours. Images are checked locally with OCR, without an external
            OCR service, and the downloaded bytes are not archived.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-white/90">Presence</strong> (online status and active platforms) is cached in memory for{' '}
            <code>/userinfo</code>, the online count in <code>/serverinfo</code>, and aggregate online, idle and
            do-not-disturb counts. Atlas stores no individual presence history. Aggregate presence counts are retained
            in Prometheus for up to one year. There is no per-user tracking opt-out.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-white/90">Voice states</strong> are used to count occupants of configured voice
            channels. Aggregate counts are retained in Prometheus for up to one year; Atlas does not record voice audio.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-white/90">Usernames, display names and avatars</strong> are shown in replies and logs as
            Discord provides them; structured user references in the database use IDs. Submitted or configured text may
            also contain names or other identifiers.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="space-y-3">
        <h2 class="mb-3 text-xl font-semibold text-white">Ko-fi Supporters</h2>
        <p>
          When you support AtlasOS on Ko-fi, Ko-fi notifies Atlas and Atlas posts a thank-you message in the server with
          the amount and, only if you made your support public on Ko-fi, your Ko-fi name and message. Ko-fi's
          notification also includes other details, such as your email address; Atlas ignores them. None of this is
          stored in Atlas's database.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div id="discord-log-channels" class="space-y-3">
        <h2 class="mb-3 text-xl font-semibold text-white">Discord Log Channels</h2>
        <p>
          To moderate the server, Atlas copies some events into{' '}
          <strong class="text-white/90">restricted log channels in Discord</strong>:
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc space-y-1 pl-6">
          <li>selected content from deleted or edited messages, with attachment names where available,</li>
          <li>direct messages you send to Atlas,</li>
          <li>moderation actions and their reasons,</li>
          <li>messages removed by the scam-image filter (who posted it, where, and the matched keyword),</li>
          <li>use of Atlas's commands and level-ups, and</li>
          <li>a record that a data deletion request was carried out (with the user ID it was for).</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Log text is truncated, and earlier content may be unavailable if a message was not cached. DM logs can include
          image previews. Some bots also share the bot role, which gives them access to the logs channel. These copies
          are stored by Discord and stay there until staff delete them. They are not part of Atlas's database, so
          deleting your data with Atlas does not remove them automatically. You can ask us to remove log messages about
          you; we will do so unless we need them to handle a moderation case.
        </p>
        <p>
          Other features also create Discord copies: starboard posts, Save message bookmarks sent to the requester's
          DMs, suggestions and decision notices, translations and Jarvis replies. These copies are not removed by{' '}
          <code>/privacy delete</code>; tell us if you want us to review copies about you.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="space-y-3">
        <h2 class="mb-3 text-xl font-semibold text-white">Third Parties</h2>
        <ul class="list-disc space-y-1 pl-6">
          <li>
            <strong class="text-white/90">Discord.</strong> Atlas runs on Discord. Everything you post is also processed
            by Discord under{' '}
            <a class="text-primary hover:underline" href="https://discord.com/privacy">
              Discord's privacy policy
            </a>
            .
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-white/90">Groq.</strong> When a member invokes{' '}
            <strong class="text-white/90">Jarvis</strong> or <strong class="text-white/90">Translate</strong>, Atlas
            sends selected text and relevant context to{' '}
            <a class="text-primary hover:underline" href="https://groq.com">
              Groq
            </a>{' '}
            over HTTPS to generate the reply. Translate can process another member's message; Jarvis can include up to
            ten messages from a reply chain. Text may contain mentions, URLs or personal information. We have enabled
            Zero Data Retention for the Groq organization used by Atlas's inference API key. Under that setting, Groq
            does not retain inference inputs or outputs for reliability or abuse monitoring. Groq retains usage metadata
            that does not contain those inputs or outputs. We do not authorize training on Discord content. Processing
            is subject to{' '}
            <a class="text-primary hover:underline" href="https://console.groq.com/docs/your-data">
              Groq's data controls
            </a>{' '}
            and{' '}
            <a class="text-primary hover:underline" href="https://console.groq.com/docs/legal/services-agreement">
              service terms
            </a>
            , including applicable legal obligations. Nothing is sent to Groq unless a member invokes one of these
            features.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-white/90">Infrastructure providers.</strong> AtlasOS operates the bot on hosted
            infrastructure. The hosting provider supplies the underlying server. Cloudflare handles traffic to our
            public endpoints and authenticated monitoring services through Access and Tunnel, including monitoring
            results containing Discord IDs.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-white/90">Search links.</strong> <code>/lmgtfy</code> and a configured reaction can
            create a link containing a query or text from a member's message. The bot posts the link in Discord;
            following it sends that text to letmegooglethat.com and may lead to a search provider.
          </li>
          <li>We share data for the features and infrastructure described in this policy, and never sell it.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="space-y-3">
        <h2 class="mb-3 text-xl font-semibold text-white">Metrics</h2>
        <p>
          Atlas exports operational and community metrics (for example member counts, message counts per channel and
          per-moderator action counts) to a private <strong class="text-white/90">Prometheus</strong> and{' '}
          <strong class="text-white/90">Grafana</strong> instance operated by AtlasOS. Only AtlasOS staff can view them.
          Some series are labelled with Discord user IDs: <strong class="text-white/90">moderators' IDs</strong> on
          moderation action counts and <strong class="text-white/90">invite creators' IDs</strong> on invite usage.
          Metrics are kept for <strong class="text-white/90">up to one year</strong>. Administrators attempt to delete
          the series labelled with your user ID and verify the result. Related moderator series are retained when
          moderation records are kept. Aggregate counts cannot be separated back into individual contributions.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="space-y-3">
        <h2 class="mb-3 text-xl font-semibold text-white">Storage &amp; Security</h2>
        <p>
          Atlas's database, metrics, persistent application logs and local backups are stored{' '}
          <strong class="text-white/90">encrypted at rest</strong> on a LUKS2 volume on infrastructure operated by
          AtlasOS. Administrative access is restricted to the team members who run the services. This encryption
          protects stored volumes; it does not make a running server inaccessible to its administrators or control
          storage held by Discord or other providers.
        </p>
        <p>
          Before database migrations, Atlas can create a backup in <code>/data/backups</code>; it keeps the three most
          recent copies. A separate infrastructure dump tool can create copies in <code>/srv/vault/dumps</code> and
          keeps the ten most recent dump sets. Rotation happens when new copies are created, without a fixed maximum
          age. Deleted data may remain in these copies until they are removed or replaced. Deleting database rows does
          not guarantee immediate physical erasure of old database or journal bytes.
        </p>
        <p>
          Application logs are intended to contain operational events, errors and Discord IDs rather than message
          bodies; exception text can include details supplied by underlying services. They have a configured 30-day
          retention period, with routine deletion processing afterward. Privacy deletion does not purge these logs, and
          creates an operational record that the request was carried out.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="space-y-3">
        <h2 class="mb-3 text-xl font-semibold text-white">Moderation Records</h2>
        <p>
          If you ask us to delete your data, we delete your moderation cases as well, unless keeping them is necessary
          to protect the server, for example to prevent someone who was banned from returning under a new account or to
          handle an ongoing report. If we keep moderation records, we tell you which records we kept and why. Unless
          moderation records are retained, your user ID is replaced with <code>Deleted</code> where you acted as a
          moderator, and your moderator counters are merged into a total labelled <code>Deleted</code>. When moderation
          records are retained, these identifiers and related metric series are retained too.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="space-y-3">
        <h2 class="mb-3 text-xl font-semibold text-white">Your Choices &amp; Rights</h2>
        <p>You can ask us to:</p>
        <ul class="list-disc space-y-1 pl-6">
          <li>
            <strong class="text-white/90">see</strong> what Atlas stores about you,
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-white/90">correct</strong> something that is wrong (for example a moderation case
            reason),
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-white/90">delete</strong> your data,
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong class="text-white/90">stop</strong> using your data for a specific purpose.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Contact us at{' '}
          <a class="text-primary hover:underline" href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>
            {CONTACT_EMAIL}
          </a>{' '}
          or talk to a support representative in{' '}
          <a class="text-primary hover:underline" href="https://discord.gg/atlasos">
            discord.gg/atlasos
          </a>
          . We may ask you to confirm the request from your Discord account so we know it is yours. We respond{' '}
          <strong class="text-white/90">within 30 days</strong>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Administrators use <code>/privacy info</code> to see counts of stored database rows and{' '}
          <code>/privacy delete</code> to remove your level, per-user Wrapped counts, invite attribution, starboard
          database entries and suggestions, and replace your ID with <code>Deleted</code> where you created invites,
          invited other members or decided suggestions. Moderation handling follows the exceptions above. The command
          then attempts to purge matching Prometheus series; administrators check the result and follow up on failures
          separately.
        </p>
        <p>
          The command does not remove Discord copies, backups, application logs, Wrapped message links or references in
          arbitrary text. Staff separately review these parts of your request and explain any retained records. There is
          no automatic per-user exclusion from future collection. If you stay in or rejoin the server, Atlas starts
          collecting the data described above again as you use it.
        </p>
        <p>
          Leaving the server does not delete your data automatically: your level is kept so it can be restored if you
          come back. Ask us if you want it deleted.
        </p>
        <p>
          Atlas is part of a Discord server, so it is subject to Discord's minimum age requirements. We do not knowingly
          collect data about anyone below them.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="space-y-3">
        <h2 class="mb-3 text-xl font-semibold text-white">Changes to This Policy</h2>
        <p>
          We will update this policy when Atlas starts collecting something new or uses data differently. The effective
          date at the top always shows the latest version, and significant changes are announced in the AtlasOS server.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</DefaultLayout>
